1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:06,829
Curry and Howard noticed that the rules
for building proofs are the same as the
rules for building programs.

2
00:00:06,829 --> 00:00:11,128
This episode shows that correspondence
using code you already write.

3
00:00:12,033 --> 00:00:13,791
Start with a function.

4
00:00:13,791 --> 00:00:17,946
The Python version takes an integer and
returns one.

5
00:00:17,946 --> 00:00:22,420
The Lean version takes a natural number
and returns one.

6
00:00:22,420 --> 00:00:26,655
Look at the shape: an input type, and an
output type.

7
00:00:26,655 --> 00:00:29,531
In logic, that shape is implication.

8
00:00:29,531 --> 00:00:35,204
A proof that A implies B is a function
from proofs of A to proofs of B.

9
00:00:36,233 --> 00:00:38,426
A pair holds two values.

10
00:00:38,426 --> 00:00:41,715
Python has a record with two fields.

11
00:00:41,715 --> 00:00:45,644
Lean has a product type, written A times
B.

12
00:00:45,644 --> 00:00:50,121
In logic, the same shape is conjunction: A
and B.

13
00:00:50,121 --> 00:00:55,695
A proof of A and B is a pair: a proof of
A, and a proof of B.

14
00:00:56,700 --> 00:01:00,730
A tagged union holds one of several
values.

15
00:01:00,730 --> 00:01:03,354
Python has unions and enums.

16
00:01:03,354 --> 00:01:06,915
Lean has a sum type, written A plus B.

17
00:01:06,915 --> 00:01:10,476
In logic, that is disjunction: A or B.

18
00:01:10,476 --> 00:01:17,598
A proof of A or B is a value carrying a
tag that says which side you proved.

19
00:01:18,600 --> 00:01:20,373
Here is the intuition.

20
00:01:20,373 --> 00:01:28,832
A product multiplies: two choices for the
first field, and three for the second,
give six possible pairs.

21
00:01:28,832 --> 00:01:35,681
A sum adds: two cases on the left, and
three on the right, give five possible
values.

22
00:01:35,681 --> 00:01:37,453
And is multiplication.

23
00:01:37,453 --> 00:01:38,662
Or is addition.

24
00:01:39,667 --> 00:01:42,300
Put the two columns side by side.

25
00:01:42,300 --> 00:01:45,892
Propositions on the left, types on the
right.

26
00:01:45,892 --> 00:01:48,207
A proof of A and B is a pair.

27
00:01:48,207 --> 00:01:51,080
A proof of A or B is a tagged value.

28
00:01:51,080 --> 00:01:54,033
A proof of A implies B is a function.

29
00:01:54,033 --> 00:01:57,066
True is the unit type, with one value.

30
00:01:57,066 --> 00:01:59,859
False is the empty type, with none.

31
00:02:01,067 --> 00:02:05,255
So the programs you would write anyway are
the proofs.

32
00:02:05,255 --> 00:02:08,823
The identity function proves that A
implies A.

33
00:02:08,823 --> 00:02:13,245
Taking the first component proves that A
and B implies A.

34
00:02:13,245 --> 00:02:17,666
Tagging a value on the left proves that A
implies A or B.

35
00:02:17,666 --> 00:02:21,312
Nothing new is needed: the terms already
exist.

36
00:02:22,533 --> 00:02:25,948
The difference is what the machine does
with them.

37
00:02:25,948 --> 00:02:31,206
Python annotations are documentation:
nothing checks the call before it runs.

38
00:02:31,206 --> 00:02:34,894
Lean checks the term against the type, and
rejects it.

39
00:02:34,894 --> 00:02:38,992
When the type is a proposition, that check
is a proof check.

40
00:02:38,992 --> 00:02:41,655
The compiler becomes the proof checker.

41
00:02:42,867 --> 00:02:45,486
Same shapes, different guarantees.

42
00:02:45,486 --> 00:02:50,416
That is the whole trick: you have been
writing proofs all along.

